items.body:
You can join strings with `+`:

~~~
const food = "pizza";
console.log("I love " + food + "!");
~~~

A neater way is a **template literal**: wrap the string in backticks and put variables inside `${ }`:

~~~
console.log(`I love ${food}!`);
~~~

Strings also have useful properties and methods, like `food.length` (5) and `food.toUpperCase()` ("PIZZA").

A **function** is a reusable recipe. You give it inputs (**parameters**), and it can give back a result with `return`.

~~~
function double(n) {
  return n * 2;
}

console.log(double(4));  // 8
console.log(double(10)); // 20
~~~

Defining a function doesn't run it. You **call** it by writing its name followed by parentheses. Once `return` runs, the function stops.

